<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bóndi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as húsbóndi, bændr, karl</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bóndi (plural bændr) was the Old Norse name for the free farmers and craftsmen who formed the core of society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>, a widespread middle class. They were free men with rights such as bearing weapons and, as farm-owning landlords, joining the Thing. The poem Rígsthula tells how the god Ríg fathered three classes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">thralls</a>, the karls (or bændr) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarls</a>. Besides farming, bændr were sailors, merchants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>, and further north hunters and fishermen. In the Icelandic Commonwealth a bóndi was the formal follower of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothi">goði</a>, whose will influenced his vote at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/althing">Althing</a>, under laws collected in the Grágás.</p></div>
